+# PGL block-stabilizer witnesses from arXiv:2408.14714
+
+| Profile field | Value |
+|---|---|
+| Track | **B — no-tool compression** |
+| Native domain | algebra |
+| Object regime | finite field |
+| Computational core | linear algebra |
+| Certificate | 2×2 matrix certificate over `F_p` |
+| Intended intuition | invariant: the finite-field barycenter reveals the hidden affine translation |
+| Domain essentiality | native |
+| Reduction | none |
+
+This generator turns Paul Tricot's [*On 3-designs from PGL(2,q)*](https://arxiv.org/abs/2408.14714) into a stabilizer-witness problem.  The solver receives a block on the projective line over a prime field.  It is an affine image of the paper's multiplicative-subgroup block and the solver must exhibit a nonidentity involution in `PGL(2,p)` that preserves it.  The witness is a normalized trace-zero matrix `[[a,b],[1,-a]]`; verification checks its entries, determinant, and exact action on every block point.  It never reads the planted answer.
+
+## Why this is Track B
+
+Section 2 defines the projective line, fractional-linear action, block orbit, and stabilizer.  Lemma 12 proves that `B=<theta^r>` has dihedral stabilizer `D_(2k)` when `k-1` does not divide `q`; Theorem 13 obtains the resulting 3-design parameter.  The generator takes `q=p` prime and an odd prime `k<p` dividing `p-1`, so `k-1` cannot divide `p`.  It samples the subgroup and an affine disguise first, then carries the paper's inversion witness through that map.
+
+An efficient algorithm exists and is disclosed.  A projective map is determined by three points, so generic exact stabilizer recovery enumerates `k(k-1)(k-2)` possible ordered image triples and checks the induced maps: `O(k^4)` field operations in the worst case.  Across eight hard-preset runs, the bundled implementation solved 8/8, testing 9,334 maps and 300,724 instrumented abstract field operations on average in 0.055 seconds.  The compact route uses `sum(B)=k*t`: recover the hidden translation `t`, select any displayed `x`, and conjugate the subgroup reflection through `x-t`.  It needs at most 135 exact operations at `k=127`, but has to be recognized and carried out without finite-field tools.
+
+Asking for the paper's displayed value of `lambda` was rejected at triage: Theorem 13 gives it by a direct two-case formula.  The exceptional `k-1|q` regime was also avoided because Lemma 12 gives a much larger subfield stabilizer there.
+
+## Worked demo
+
+For `make_instance(n=5, modulus_min=31, prime_spread=0, seed=0)`, the complete instance data are `p=31`, `k=5`, and block
+
+```text
+17 16 21 8 6
+```
+
+The field sum is `68 = 6 (mod 31)`, and `5^-1 = 25`, so the hidden translation is `t=26`.  Using the first point gives the valid answer
+
+```text
+<answer>[[26,25],[1,5]]</answer>
+```
+
+`verify(inst, [[26,25],[1,5]])` returns `(True, "ok")`.  Replacing `25` by the out-of-range entry `31` returns `(False, "matrix entries must lie in the inclusive range 0..30")`.  This smallest setting is genuinely hand-solvable; its bounded matrix language has only 930 candidates and exactly five valid reflections.
+
+## Difficulty presets
+
+The field shown is the deterministic example at seed `24680`; other seeds choose other primes in the same scale.  The answer always has four entries.
+
+| Preset | `k` | Example `p` | Candidate matrices | Intended operations | Status |
+|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---|
+| demo | 5 | 31 | 930 | 13 | hand example; not hardened |
+| easy | 23 | 722,063 | 521,374,253,906 | 31 | oracle run blocked before scoring |
+| medium | 61 | 21,766,753 | 473,791,514,396,256 | 69 | not reached |
+| hard | 127 | 1,195,978,051 | 1,430,363,497,277,780,550 | 135 | selected shipping preset; local gates pass |
+
+## Gate results
+
+| Gate | Result | Measurement |
+|---|---|---|
+| G1 | pass | 16/16 planted witnesses verified across all presets |
+| G2 | pass | five corruptions rejected with five distinct reasons |
+| G3 | pass | fenced JSON plus surrounding prose round-tripped; answer is JSON-native |
+| G4 | pass | 0/200,000 structure-aware guesses; constrained space `1,430,363,497,277,780,550` |
+| G5 | pass | theorem-backed hard count 127, density `8.879e-17`; demo brute-force count 5; baseline measured above |
+| G6 | pass | all five attacks 0/8; reference algorithm 8/8 as Track B requires |
+| G7 | pass | doubling `n` produced `k=257`; space grew while the witness stayed at four atoms |
+| G8 | pass | 80/80 key-invariance and 80/80 carried-certificate checks; 20/20 unrelated seeds distinct |
+| G9(c) | pass | 37 characters, about 10 tokens, four atoms, 135 intended operations |
+
+The G6 failures were: smallest point as center, ordinary-integer barycenter, swap-first-pair/fix-third, reverse-first-triple, and 256 uniform involution restarts.  Random point order and uniformly sampled affine parameters remove positional and magnitude signatures.
+
+## Oracle loop and G9 diagnostics
+
+The required harness was run, but OpenRouter rejected every request with HTTP 403, `Key limit exceeded (total limit)`.  Errors do not count as model failures, so no hardness verdict is claimed and this result is **not submission-ready until those script-owned runs are repeated with working quota**.
+
+| Run | Preset | Valid attempts | Errors | Solved | Conclusion |
+|---|---|---:|---:|---:|---|
+| bare | easy | 0 | 4 | 0 | pool unreachable; ladder not scored |
+| structural hint | hard | 0 | 4 | 0 | diagnostic unavailable |
+| placebo hint | hard | 0 | 4 | 0 | diagnostic unavailable |
+
+Because both diagnostic denominators are zero, `hinted - placebo` is undefined, not evidence that the hint had no effect.  The partial transcripts preserve all twelve 403 records so the infrastructure failure is auditable rather than silently scored as hardness.
+
+## Use
+
+```python
+from gen_2408_14714 import DIFFICULTY, make_instance, render, parse_answer, verify
+
+inst = make_instance(seed=7, **DIFFICULTY["hard"])
+print(render(inst))
+answer = parse_answer("<answer>[[1,2],[1,3]]</answer>")
+print(verify(inst, answer))
+```
+
+From the repository root, after a successful hardening rerun:
+
+```bash
+python3 results/2408.14714/gen_2408_14714.py
+python3 scripts/harden.py results/2408.14714/gen_2408_14714.py
+bash scripts/emit.sh 2408.14714 20 hard
+```
+
+## Caveats
+
+This is deliberately not a complexity-theoretic hardness claim: the reference algorithm is fast on a computer.  Recognition of the barycenter identity makes the problem short, which is the intended Track B phenomenon.  The 0/200,000 figure concerns a uniform prior over already well-formed trace-zero `c=1` involutions; it does not bound algebraically informed guesses.  No external computer-algebra stabilizer package, discrete-log method, or alternative moment-invariant attack was tested.
+
+For fixed `(p,k)`, every generated block is in the same `PGL` orbit, so `canonical_key` correctly collapses all affine disguises; diversity comes from seed-selected prime fields, not from pretending coordinate changes are new problems.  The 64-bit deterministic primality routine covers the shipped parameter range.  Most importantly, the missing multi-vendor verdict is an external blocker: rerun all three arms before treating `SHIPPING_DIFFICULTY="hard"` as corpus evidence.
