-This generator turns Paul Tricot's [*On 3-designs from PGL(2,q)*](https://arxiv.org/abs/2408.14714) into a stabilizer-witness problem.  The solver receives a block on the projective line over a prime field.  It is an affine image of the paper's multiplicative-subgroup block and the solver must exhibit a nonidentity involution in `PGL(2,p)` that preserves it.  The witness is a normalized trace-zero matrix `[[a,b],[1,-a]]`; verification checks its entries, determinant, and exact action on every block point.  It never reads the planted answer.
+## Problem family and provenance
 
-## Why this is Track B
+[Paul Tricot, *On 3-designs from PGL(2,q)*](https://arxiv.org/abs/2408.14714) studies the action of fractional-linear transformations on subsets of the projective line. Section 2 supplies the exact native objects and the orbit–stabilizer relation. Lemma 12 proves that, for the multiplicative-subgroup block `B=<theta^r>` in the non-subfield regime, its full stabilizer is dihedral; Theorem 13 derives the resulting 3-design parameter.
 
-Section 2 defines the projective line, fractional-linear action, block orbit, and stabilizer.  Lemma 12 proves that `B=<theta^r>` has dihedral stabilizer `D_(2k)` when `k-1` does not divide `q`; Theorem 13 obtains the resulting 3-design parameter.  The generator takes `q=p` prime and an odd prime `k<p` dividing `p-1`, so `k-1` cannot divide `p`.  It samples the subgroup and an affine disguise first, then carries the paper's inversion witness through that map.
+An instance gives a prime `p` and an unordered affine image `S=sH+t` of the unique order-`k` subgroup `H` of `F_p*`. The solver must return a concrete nonidentity involution in `PGL(2,p)` that preserves `S`, as a normalized 2-by-2 matrix `[[a,b],[1,d]]`. Verification checks the shape, finite-field trace and determinant, applies the fractional-linear map to every point, and compares exact sets. It never reads the planted answer.
 
-An efficient algorithm exists and is disclosed.  A projective map is determined by three points, so generic exact stabilizer recovery enumerates `k(k-1)(k-2)` possible ordered image triples and checks the induced maps: `O(k^4)` field operations in the worst case.  Across eight hard-preset runs, the bundled implementation solved 8/8, testing 9,334 maps and 300,724 instrumented abstract field operations on average in 0.055 seconds.  The compact route uses `sum(B)=k*t`: recover the hidden translation `t`, select any displayed `x`, and conjugate the subgroup reflection through `x-t`.  It needs at most 135 exact operations at `k=127`, but has to be recognized and carried out without finite-field tools.
+Generation is by transformation of a known instance. The generator first constructs `H`, samples `s,t`, and carries an inversion of `H` through `x -> sx+t`; it does not search for a stabilizer.
 
-Asking for the paper's displayed value of `lambda` was rejected at triage: Theorem 13 gives it by a direct two-case formula.  The exceptional `k-1|q` regime was also avoided because Lemma 12 gives a much larger subfield stabilizer there.
+## Why Track B
 
-## Worked demo
+This is deliberately not a Track A claim. A generic exact algorithm fixes three source points, enumerates their ordered image triples, constructs the unique projective map, and tests block preservation. Its worst-case cost is `O(k^3)` candidate maps and `O(k^4)` field operations. On eight hard instances it solved 8/8, as expected, after a mean 9,334.1 candidate maps and 300,724 instrumented abstract field operations; repeated local wall-clock means ranged from 0.05 to 0.54 seconds depending on concurrent load. The operation model treats a built-in modular inverse as one operation, so it understates bit work.
 
-For `make_instance(n=5, modulus_min=31, prime_spread=0, seed=0)`, the complete instance data are `p=31`, `k=5`, and block
+The compressed route uses the missing coefficient of `x^(k-1)` in `x^k-1`: the elements of `H` sum to zero, hence the finite-field barycenter of `S` is `t`. With `t` and any displayed point `x0`, conjugated inversion is represented by `[[t,(x0-t)^2-t^2],[1,-t]]`. At the hard preset this is conservatively counted as 135 exact field operations. It fits the cap but is unpleasant to execute unaided on 127 ten-digit residues. Asking only for the design parameter would be trivial because Theorem 13 displays it; this family instead asks for an executable stabilizer witness. The exceptional `k-1 | p` subfield case is avoided: here `p` and `k` are distinct primes, `k | p-1`, and therefore `1 < k-1 < p` cannot divide `p`.
 
